## Why Relying Solely on `.env` Files Is Problematic

| Issue | Why It Matters | Real‑world Impact |
|-------|----------------|-------------------|
| **Plain‑text storage** | Secrets are stored unencrypted on disk. Anyone with read access to the filesystem (e.g., a compromised container, a poorly configured backup, an insider, CI runner) can retrieve them. | Leakage of database passwords, cloud keys, payment‑processor keys, etc. |
| **Accidental commit** | Developers may unintentionally `git add .env*` and push to a public repository. History can be hard to purge. | Massive data breach, credential rotation costs, reputational damage. |
| **Environment drift** | Production uses a copy of the same file, making it hard to rotate or audit per‑environment secrets. | Stale or over‑privileged credentials persist. |
| **Lack of audit/rotation** | No built‑in mechanism to rotate secrets or to audit access. | Long‑lived credentials become a high‑value target for attackers. |
| **No fine‑grained IAM** | All services share the same set of keys, violating the principle of least privilege. | A single compromised service can access everything (DB, AWS, Stripe, Anthropic, etc.). |

