## Why a plain `.env` file is Not Ideal for Production

| Issue | Explanation |
|-------|-------------|
| **Plain‑text storage** | Anyone with read access to the repository or the deployment host can view the secrets. |
| **No rotation / audit** | Updating the value requires a manual change and a redeploy; you lose history of who changed what. |
| **No access‑control granularity** | All services that can read the file see *every* variable, even those they don’t need. |
| **Risk of accidental commit** | Developers sometimes add `.env` to version control, leaking credentials. |
| **No integration with cloud‑native IAM** | You miss out on the ability to grant temporary, scoped permissions (e.g., an AWS IAM role that can read a secret only when a specific task runs). |

Because of those reasons, most production‑grade Node.js services move secret retrieval out of a static file and use a **managed secrets manager**.

