## Why loading secrets from a `.env` file is a risk

| Risk | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Accidental commit** | `.env` files are easy to commit by mistake (especially when they contain real keys). Once in version control they become part of the audit trail and can be scraped from public forks or leaks. |
| **Static at rest** | The file lives on disk in plain text; anyone with filesystem access (including a compromised container, a mis‑configured backup, or a malicious insider) can read it. |
| **No rotation / audit** | Secrets in a file are not versioned or rotated automatically; you must manually replace them and redeploy. |
| **Lack of fine‑grained access control** | Everyone who can start the process can read *all* secrets, even if a component only needs one of them. |
| **Inconsistent environments** | Development, staging, CI, and production may all read the same file, leading to “test keys in prod” or vice‑versa. |

