### 2️⃣  Load secrets at runtime from a secrets manager

#### Example: AWS Secrets Manager (Node.js SDK v3)

```js
// secret-loader.js
const { SecretsManagerClient, GetSecretValueCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-secrets-manager");

// The name of the secret that holds a JSON object with all required keys
const SECRET_NAME = process.env.APP_SECRETS_NAME; // e.g. "myapp/prod"

const client = new SecretsManagerClient({ region: process.env.AWS_REGION });

async function loadSecrets() {
  const command = new GetSecretValueCommand({ SecretId: SECRET_NAME });
  const response = await client.send(command);
  // Assume secret is stored as a JSON string: {"DATABASE_URL":"...","JWT_SECRET":"..."}
  const secretString = response.SecretString;
  if (!secretString) throw new Error("SecretString was empty");
  const secretObj = JSON.parse(secretString);
  // Populate process.env so the rest of the app can read it normally
  Object.assign(process.env, secretObj);
}

module.exports = { loadSecrets };
```

**Bootstrapping in `app.js`**

```js
// Load secrets *before* any other modules need them
const { loadSecrets } = require("./secret-loader");

// Async IIFE to start the server after secrets are fetched
(async () => {
  await loadSecrets();          // pulls from AWS Secrets Manager
  // … the rest of the existing app setup (express, helmet, etc.) …
  require("./server");          // move the server start logic into its own file
})();
```

*If you use another provider (Azure Key Vault, GCP Secret Manager, HashiCorp Vault, etc.) replace the SDK call accordingly – the pattern is the same: fetch, parse, **populate `process.env`**.*

### 3️⃣  Keep a fallback for local development

When you run the app on a dev machine you may still want an `.env` file, but it **must never** be checked in.

```js
