## Why Loading Secrets Directly from a `.env` File Is a Risk

| Issue | Why It Matters |
|-------|----------------|
| **File‑system exposure** – The `.env` file lives on disk and can be read by any user/process that has read permission on the directory. If the container image is built with the file baked in, the secret becomes part of the image and can be pulled from a registry. |
| **Version‑control leakage** – Accidentally committing `.env` (or a copy) to Git leaks secrets to every clone, CI logs, PR reviewers, etc. |
| **Static secret rotation** – Changing a secret means updating the file everywhere it is deployed, rebuilding images, and redeploying. Automation becomes harder. |
| **Auditing & revocation** – Centralised secret stores (AWS Secrets Manager, Vault, GCP Secret Manager, etc.) provide audit logs, fine‑grained IAM, and short‑lived dynamic credentials. A plain text file provides none of that. |
| **Environment‑specific values** – In CI/CD pipelines you may have separate test/production secrets; mixing them in files leads to “leak‑through” when the wrong file is packaged. |

